title: The -hard error does not name the @INC entry that failed
updated: 2026-10-25T05:26:40Z
---

In -hard mode a missing directory aborts the import with

    die $dir . " of @INC doesn't exist" unless -d $dir;

The message names the absolute path that was missing, but not the @INC entry it came from. A caller whose @INC holds five relative entries cannot tell which one is broken from the message alone, and the absolute path is not what the caller wrote in their configuration.

Work:
- make the message name the offending @INC entry as well as the absolute path it resolved to
- keep the failure inside import and keep it fatal
- add a test that pins the new wording and that fails without it. The current suite does not pin the message at all, so nothing catches a change here
- the change is user visible: it wants a Changes entry in the same commit

